    "lawCode": "Add §3444, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S817A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nfood service in funeral establishments\n\nPURPOSE:  To give families of deceased individuals the option of\nconsuming food and beverages in a funeral home.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new Public Health Law section § 3444 to provide that\na funeral establishment may provide space, facilities, equipment or\nsupplies for the preparation, sale, service, distribution, or\nconsumption of food or beverages in the funeral establishments to or\nby persons in the funeral establishment for the purpose of arranging\nfor, attending, or participating in the care and preparation of a body\nof a deceased person for disposition, mourning, a funeral or memorial\nceremony or service, or an open house or similar community function.\n\nActivities under this section may include the services of a caterer\nnot owned by or affiliated with the funeral establishment. Employees\nof the funeral establishment are limited to preparing nonalcoholic\nbeverages and may serve food or non-alcoholic beverages under this\nsection.\n\nFood and beverage service would not be allowed in the embalming or\npreparation rooms. The Commissioner of Health is directed to make\nregulations necessary to implement this act.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Currently 48 states do not prohibit food and beverage\nin funeral homes. For several decades, NYS regulations have banned the\nserving of any type of food or beverage in a funeral home, including\nwater.\n\nThis legislation would allow the option of funeral homes serving food\nand non-alcoholic beverages in connection with a funeral. It further\nspecifies the conditions for food and beverage service. It would allow\nthe serving of incidental food (baked goods, sandwiches, snacks and\nplatters) and non-alcoholic beverages. It allows the services of a\ncaterer not owned by or affiliated with the funeral establishment.\nFuneral Home employees are specifically limited to preparing beverages\nand may serve food or beverages. This legislation would allow funeral\nhomes to work in tandem with local restaurants/caterers in instances\nwhen food and/or beverages are requested by the deceased's family. The\nCommissioner of Health is authorized and directed to establish\nregulations to implement these provisions. Food or beverage service\nwould be prohibited in a funeral home's embalming and preparation\nrooms.\n\nToday visiting or calling hours can run for several hours. Funeral\nhomes would have the option of being able to provide refreshments on\nsite such as baked goods, water and coffee which would provide\nnourishment to sustain the family of the deceased at their time of\nneed. This would be especially helpful for elderly family members\nduring these long hours.\n\n\nThe option of funeral homes providing food and non-alcoholic beverages\nwould also be helpful for families in rural areas where other food\noptions are not easily accessible.\n\nToday's funeral homes serve a variety of cultures with differing\nfuneral or memorial service expectations and preferences. As well as\nstrictly solemn events, the deceased may be remembered through more of\na life celebration. This bill would allow families the option of\nchoosing to have light food and beverages served to mourners.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  2014: S. 7747B Passed Senate; A. 9950 Referred\nto Health\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  180 days after it becomes a law.\n\n",
