    "lawCode": "Add §3444, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S817B\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto food service in funeral establishments\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo give families of deceased individuals the option of consuming food\nand beverages in a funeral home.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 adds a new Public Health Law section § 3444 to provide that\na funeral establishment may provide space, facilities, equipment or\nsupplies for the preparation, sale, service, distribution, or\nconsumption of food or nonalcoholic beverages, limited to incidental\nrefreshments, in the funeral establishment to or by persons in the\nfuneral establishment for the purpose of a funeral or memorial\nceremony or service.\n\nActivities under this section may include the services of a caterer\nnot owned by or affiliated with the funeral establishment. Employees\nof the funeral establishment and catering employees are limited to\npreparing non-alcoholic beverages and may serve incidental food or\nnonalcoholic beverages under this section.\n\nFood and beverage service would not be allowed in the embalming or\npreparation rooms. The Commissioner of Health is directed to make\nregulations necessary to implement this act.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently 48 states do not prohibit food and beverage in funeral\nhomes. For several decades, NYS regulations have banned the serving of\nany type of food or beverage in a funeral home, including water.\n\nThis legislation would allow the option of funeral homes serving food\nand nonalcoholic beverages in connection with a funeral. It further\nspecifies the conditions for food and beverage service. It would allow\nthe serving of incidental food (baked goods, sandwiches, snacks and\nplatters) and non-alcoholic beverages. It allows the services of a\ncaterer not owned by or affiliated with the funeral establishment.\nFuneral Home employees and catering employees are specifically limited\nto preparing beverages and may serve incidental refreshments or\nnonalcoholic beverages. This legislation would allow funeral homes to\nwork in tandem with local restaurants/caterers in instances when food\nand/or beverages are requested by the deceased's family. The\nCommissioner of Health is authorized and directed to establish\nregulations to implement these provisions. Food or beverage service\nwould be prohibited in a funeral home's embalming and preparation\nrooms.\n\nToday visiting or calling hours can run for several hours. Funeral\nhomes would have the option of being able to provide refreshments on\nsite such as baked goods, water and coffee which would provide\nnourishment to sustain the family of the deceased at their time of\nneed. This would be especially helpful for elderly family members\nduring these long hours.\n\nThe option of funeral homes providing incidental food and nonalcoholic\nbeverages would also be helpful for families in rural areas where\nother food options are not easily accessible.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015: S817A Advanced to Third Reading\n2014: S.7747B Passed Senate; A. 9950 Referred to Health\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nnone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n180 days after it becomes a law.\n",
